WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.418
Picture a small program that counts every card's payments.

2
00:00:06.418 --> 00:00:10.649
When a card has paid ten times, it emails the owner: was this you?

3
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:13.478
One busy Saturday its machine loses power.

4
00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:17.759
A minute later it's back, carrying on from where it last wrote down.

5
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:24.672
On Monday, a customer calls: she got the same email twice, and the counts show some payments twice too.

6
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:30.057
So how do you save the place of a program that never stops, so that nothing counts twice?

7
00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:36.627
Picture yourself at a desk, counting receipts from two shoeboxes, with a tally per card on a sheet.

8
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:41.977
Every so often, you write a note: the tally, and how far you've got in each box.

9
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:45.229
After an interruption, you start again from the note.

10
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.106
But the note only works if the tally and the two places describe the same moment.

11
00:00:50.106 --> 00:00:55.973
If the tally includes five receipts the note says you haven't reached, you'll count those five twice.

12
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:03.607
A snapshot like that, where every record counted comes before the saved places and none after, is called consistent.

13
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:05.478
The easy way is to stop.

14
00:01:05.478 --> 00:01:10.983
Stop reading both boxes, finish what's in your hands, write the note, start again.

15
00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:12.057
It's consistent.

16
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:17.089
But a real program does this every few seconds, and every time, everything stops.

17
00:01:17.089 --> 00:01:20.899
Wouldn't it be nice to take the note while the counting goes on?

18
00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:25.269
Flink is a program that runs stream jobs across many machines.

19
00:01:25.269 --> 00:01:28.113
A job is a chain of steps called operators.

20
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.597
Sources read the input, here two Kafka logs of payments: those are the shoeboxes.

21
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.104
A counter keeps the count per card: that's the tally.

22
00:01:37.104 --> 00:01:40.533
A sink, the last step, writes results out to the world.

23
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:45.817
Between two steps runs a channel, a queue where records wait in the order they were sent.

24
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:51.667
Every few seconds, Flink saves every operator's state and every source's offset.

25
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:55.200
That saved snapshot is called a checkpoint: the note.

26
00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:59.153
Here's the trick, from Chandy and Lamport in 1985.

27
00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:02.028
Slip a coloured card into each shoebox.

28
00:02:02.028 --> 00:02:03.871
Flink calls it a barrier.

29
00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:14.185
It starts checkpoint 1: each source writes down its offset, 7 and 7, and puts barrier B1 into its channel, behind the records it has already sent.

30
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:20.951
Barriers never overtake records, so B1 splits each channel into before the checkpoint and after it.

31
00:02:20.951 --> 00:02:22.154
And nothing stops.

32
00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:23.825
The sources keep reading.

33
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.283
B1 reaches the counter on input 0 first.

34
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:30.705
Input 1 still has a record ahead of its B1.

35
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.803
Two new records from source 0 are queuing behind the barrier.

36
00:02:34.803 --> 00:02:36.659
Should the counter read them?

37
00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:37.683
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.188
Let's steelman yes.

39
00:02:42.188 --> 00:02:45.305
Reading never stops, which is the whole point.

40
00:02:45.305 --> 00:02:48.152
But those two records come after offset 7.

41
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:53.626
Count them now, save, and after a crash the source rewinds to 7 and counts them again.

42
00:02:53.626 --> 00:02:56.027
That's the five receipts from the desk.

43
00:02:56.233 --> 00:03:00.144
So the counter blocks input 0, and keeps reading input 1.

44
00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:07.787
At the desk, you reach the coloured card in box A, so you stop taking from box A until you reach the card in box B.

45
00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.925
This waiting is called alignment.

46
00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:11.970
B1 arrives on input 1.

47
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:20.152
The counter has counted exactly 14 records: 7 from each source, 7 plus 7, the offsets in the note.

48
00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:25.567
It saves its state, sends one B1 on to the sink, and unblocks input 0.

49
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:27.289
B1 reaches the sink.

50
00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:35.395
The sink has held its 14 updates in an open transaction, a batch that nobody outside can see until it's committed.

51
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.537
Every operator has now saved its part, so checkpoint 1 is complete, and the sink commits.

52
00:03:41.537 --> 00:03:43.272
The world sees 14 updates.

53
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:44.893
Then the machine dies.

54
00:03:44.893 --> 00:03:49.271
The counter's 17, the channels and the sink's open batch are gone.

55
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.556
The 3 updates after the checkpoint were never committed, so there's nothing to undo.

56
00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:59.604
Every operator loads checkpoint 1, and each source rewinds to offset 7.

57
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:05.084
Those records are read again, and since none were in the 14, each is counted once.

58
00:04:05.084 --> 00:04:07.920
Records processed twice, effects shown once.

59
00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:11.485
Flink calls all this asynchronous barrier snapshotting.

60
00:04:11.485 --> 00:04:17.688
Asynchronous because each operator writes its state to storage in the background while processing goes on.

61
00:04:17.867 --> 00:04:18.559
Two limits.

62
00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:28.376
The desk picture breaks: you count alone and can pause whenever you like, but Flink's operators run on different machines, so the barriers do the lining up.

63
00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:33.304
And waiting at the coloured card is still waiting; the next section measures it.

64
00:04:33.304 --> 00:04:38.692
As for the email: it left the program before the restore, and no snapshot can call it back.

65
00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:48.619
So the moral of the story: a marker in every stream splits it into before and after, and a step with two inputs waits for both markers before it saves.

66
00:04:48.833 --> 00:04:52.296
Then the count always equals the sum of the saved offsets.

67
00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:53.688
One quick check.

68
00:04:53.688 --> 00:04:56.141
A checkpoint saved offsets 9 and 7.

69
00:04:56.141 --> 00:04:59.856
How many records does the counter's saved count hold?

70
00:04:59.856 --> 00:05:00.977
Pause and think.

71
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:04.859
16.

72
00:05:04.859 --> 00:05:06.837
9 plus 7.
